//! ui/theme — what a package's design tokens mean, decided by the app.
//!
//! A library that uses tokens declares its own closed vocabulary as an
//! ordinary enum and styles itself against that; the app that uses the library
//! closes the loop at mount with one theme per library, mapping that library's
//! tokens to its own or to raw values. Exhaustiveness is the whole contract:
//! when a library adds a token, every consumer fails to compile until its
//! theme maps it (`design/ui-reactivity.md` §Design tokens).
//!
//! ## The two halves, and which package writes which
//!
//! **The package that owns a vocabulary writes a `themed`** — one small
//! function taking a mapping, `fn(Token) => Color`, and answering the `themed`
//! below applied to one binding per token: `(Token.Surface.color(),
//! f(.Surface))`, and so on down the enum. It is the one function only that
//! package can write, because only that package knows what its tokens are.
//!
//! **The app that uses the package writes the mapping**, as an ordinary
//! function whose `match` over the package's enum is the compatibility check,
//! and hands `cardlib.themed(cardTheme)` to `mount` alongside one entry for
//! every other package it uses. The worked example is in the guide's
//! standard-library page, under "Design tokens".
//!
//! A binding's left half is the token *written the way the owning package
//! writes it*, and its right half is what that token is worth. The right half
//! may itself be another token, which is how a chain — a library's token to the
//! app's token to a value — is expressed; the chain is followed once, at mount.
//!
//! ## What it costs at run time, and why that is nothing
//!
//! A token is a custom property in the stylesheet, so every class is decided at
//! compile time and no theme changes any of them. Installing a theme writes one
//! `:root` block of values; switching one rewrites the same block. That is why
//! dark mode is a `Prop<Bool>` here rather than a second stylesheet: nothing is
//! re-extracted, no element is touched, and the browser repaints from the
//! variables it already had.

from "ui/prop/lib.buri" import { Prop };
from "ui/style/lib.buri" import { Color };

/// One package's tokens, resolved to values.
///
/// Opaque: it is built by `themed` or by `switching` and read only by `mount`.
/// The field is private, so no program outside this module can build a theme
/// or take one apart — the same shape as `ui/effect`'s `Scope`, and what keeps
/// the type opaque now that an exported enum exports every variant it has.
export struct Theme(ThemeKind);

/// What a theme is, kept private so that `Theme` stays opaque.
enum ThemeKind {
  /// Each token, paired with what it is worth.
  Values([(Color, Color)]),
  /// The first list while the condition reads true, the second while it reads
  /// false. The lists are one theme each; an array is how a value refers to
  /// its own type.
  Switching(Prop<Bool>, [Theme], [Theme]),
}

/// A package's tokens, resolved.
///
/// Each binding pairs a token — written the way the owning package writes it,
/// which is `Token.Surface.color()` — with the colour it is worth. That colour
/// may be another package's token, and the chain resolves at mount.
///
/// A binding whose left half is not a token names nothing and is ignored: there
/// is no custom property for `.Rgb(0, 0, 0)` to be the value of.
export fn themed(bindings: [(Color, Color)]): Theme {
  Theme(.Values(bindings))
}

/// Whichever of two themes the condition currently picks.
///
/// This is dark mode, and it is deliberately not a second stylesheet: when the
/// condition changes, the custom-property block is written again and every
/// class on every element stays exactly as it was.
///
/// The condition is a `Prop<Bool>`, so it can be a constant, a signal the app
/// writes, or a computation over one — a media query bridged into a signal, a
/// stored preference, a switch in the page.
export fn switching(condition: Prop<Bool>, whenTrue: Theme, whenFalse: Theme): Theme {
  Theme(.Switching(condition, [whenTrue], [whenFalse]))
}
